{"id":"876808fa-56f8-49aa-b99a-368a14def408","object":"chat.completion","created":1785030514,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_by_id[nid] = node\\n        children[nid] = []\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\\n    root_id = None\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = node['id']\\n            break\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root node (parent null)')\\n    retained = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` as per the task SE-PE-01. The task is about \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" on a tree. The payload contains nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. There's also required_symbols list. The objective: reduce the tree by removing nodes that are removable, but we must preserve that all required symbols remain represented in the tree after removal. Also we cannot remove root, or a node that has retained children (i.e., if we remove a node, its children would be orphaned? Actually the description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That likely means we can only remove leaf nodes that are not required symbols? But read more: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And deeper nodes considered before shallower ones. Use node ID as tiebreak at equal depth.\n\nThe output must have removed_node_ids (list of strings, order of actual removal) and retained_node_ids (list of strings, sorted). So the algorithm: given a tree (nodes list, parent pointers), we need to decide which nodes to remove. The nodes have removable flag. We must keep nodes that are: root (id \"root\"? but root is given as node id \"root\" in examples, could be other? The payload shows root with id \"root\", but we should not assume hardcoded \"root\"; the root is the node with parent null, and also the node with symbol \"root\"? Actually in examples, root node has parent null and symbol \"root\". But required_symbols may include \"root\". So we must preserve all nodes that are either root (parent null) or are required by symbol? Actually \"Never remove the root\" means node with parent null. Also \"Never remove a required symbol\" means nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols? But careful: a node's symbol is its own; required_symbols list of symbols; we must ensure that after removals, every required symbol is still present in at least one node (presumably the nodes that are retained). Also \"Never remove a node with retained children\" - that means if we decide to keep a child, we cannot remove its parent, because that would orphan the child? Or does it mean we cannot remove a node that has children that will be retained? In tree pruning, you typically remove leaf nodes; if you remove an internal node, its children would become disconnected. So likely we only remove leaf nodes. But the description \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" suggests we process nodes from deepest to shallowest, and tentatively remove each leaf that is removable, if doing so does not cause any required symbol to be missing. Then we mark that node as removed and continue. That is typical Perses reduction: iteratively delete subtrees that are deletable while preserving interestingness (here, interestingness is that required symbols are present). The \"interestingness oracle\" is simply that all required symbols remain present in the tree after removal.\n\nThus algorithm:\n1. Build a mapping from id to node.\n2. Identify root: node with parent null.\n3. For each node, compute children list.\n4. Perform a topological order from deepest to shallowest (by depth descending, tiebreak by id string). Actually depth given; deeper nodes have larger depth.\n5. For each node in that order, if the node is removable and is not the root, and all required symbols would still be represented if we remove this node (i.e., after removal, the remaining retained nodes cover all required_symbols). Also we must ensure that no child of this node is retained? Actually if we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree implicitly? In Perses, removing a node means replacing it with its children? No, typical parse tree deletion: remove the node and its subtree. But here the instruction says \"Never remove a node with retained children\" - that implies that if any child is retained, we cannot remove the parent. So we should only remove leaf nodes (no children) or nodes whose children have already been removed? Actually if we process deepest first, we will remove leaves first. After a leaf node is removed, its parent may become a leaf if all its other children are removed. So we can then consider removing the parent. So we need to dynamically update which nodes are present. So we maintain a set of retained node ids. Initially all nodes are retained. Then for each node in order (deepest to shallowest), if it is removable, not root, and if after removing it, the set of symbols present in retained nodes still covers required_symbols, and furthermore the node has no retained children (i.e., all its children are already removed), then we remove it (add to removed list and remove from retained set). At the end, retained_node_ids is sorted list of remaining retained ids. removed_node_ids is the order we removed (list of strings). That fits.\n\nCheck example: First public case: nodes, required_symbols = [\"root\",\"s1\"]. root is id root, symbol root. n1 symbol s1, parent root. n2 symbol s2, removable true. n3 symbol s0, removable false? Actually n3 removable false, so cannot remove. n4 parent n2, symbol s1, removable true. n5 parent n3, symbol s2, removable false. n6 parent n1, symbol s0, removable true.\n\nProcess deepest first: depth 2 nodes: n4 (depth2), n5, n6. n5 not removable, skip. n4: removable, not root, children? none (leaf). If remove n4, retained symbols: root( root), n1 (s1), n2 (s2), n3 (s0), n5 (s2), n6 (s0). That includes root and s1 (n1). So condition satisfied. Also no retained children (leaf). So remove n4. n6: removable, leaf. Remove? After removing n4, symbols still have root, s1 from n1. So remove n6. Then depth 1 nodes: n1, n2, n3. n1: removable false, skip. n2: removable true, but has child n4? n4 already removed, so n2 currently has no retained children? Actually after removal n4, n2's child list initially had n4; if n4 removed, n2 has no retained children. Can we remove n2? If remove n2, symbols lost: s2 from n2. Remaining symbols: root (root), n1 (s1), n3 (s0), n5 (s2) - s2 still present in n5, and s1 present, root present. So condition holds. Remove n2. n3: removable false, skip. depth 0 root: not removable, skip. So removed = [n4, n6, n2]? Order: n4, n6, n2. Retained nodes: root, n1, n3, n5. Sort: root, n1, n3, n5. That seems plausible.\n\nSecond case: similar structure but different utilities. We'll trust algorithm.\n\nEdge cases: required_symbols might include symbols that only appear on removable nodes, but we must preserve at least one node with that symbol. So if after removal we lose that symbol, skip removal.\n\nAlso note: root may have required symbol \"root\" which is fine.\n\nTiebreak: at same depth, use node ID string as tiebreak (for order of processing). We'll sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending.\n\nImplementation: \n- Parse nodes list.\n- Build dict id->node, and children map.\n- Initially retained = set of all ids.\n- Compute required_symbols list (set for efficiency).\n- For processing order: list nodes sorted by (-depth, id).\n- For each node in that order:\n    if node[\"removable\"] is True and node[\"id\"] != root_id (parent null) and all children of node are not in retained? Actually condition: \"Never remove a node with retained children\" means if any child is still retained, we cannot remove. So check that no child id is in retained set.\n    And also check that after removal, each required symbol still appears in at least one retained node. To check efficiently, we could compute symbol counts. Since we are only removing one node at a time, we can compute if removing this node would remove a symbol that becomes missing. We can maintain a symbol count map: for each symbol, count of retained nodes with that symbol. When we remove a node, we decrement its symbol count. If after decrement, any required symbol has count 0, we cannot remove. But we also need to re-evaluate after each removal because counts change. So we can maintain a Counter for retained symbols. Initially compute from all nodes. Then for each candidate node, if its symbol count >1 (so removal won't eliminate that symbol) or if its symbol is not required? Actually required symbols must be present; if the node's symbol is not required, it's fine. But we must ensure that all required symbols remain after removal. So condition: for each required symbol, the count of that symbol in retained nodes after removal must be at least 1. Equivalent: before removal, check if the node's symbol is required and the current count for that symbol is exactly 1; if so, removal would eliminate it, so skip. Otherwise, removal is fine (provided no retained children and removable and not root). So we can check: if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols_set and symbol_counts[node[\"symbol\"]] == 1: skip.\n    But careful: what if node's symbol is not required? Then removal doesn't affect required symbols. So fine.\n    Also we must update symbol_counts after removal.\n\nProcess in order, skip if condition fails.\n\nAfter processing all, removed_node_ids is list in order of removal. retained_node_ids is sorted list of remaining ids.\n\nReturn object with those arrays.\n\nMake sure to handle case where no removals possible: removed empty list, retained all ids sorted.\n\nEdge: ensure root not removed (by condition). Also nodes with removable false should not be considered.\n\nAlso required_symbols may contain duplicates? But we treat as set.\n\nImplementation in Python.\n\nNow produce the answer JSON with single key \"implementation\" containing source code as string.\n\nWe must output exactly that JSON object, no extra text.\n\nI'll write the solve function with appropriate imports (none needed). Use standard libraries: json for output? Not needed because we return dict.\n\nBe careful: do not mutate case. We'll copy data.\n\nWrite code.\n\nPlan:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    # Build maps\n    node_by_id = {}\n    children = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_by_id[nid] = node\n        children[nid] = []\n    for node in nodes:\n        parent = node['parent']\n        if parent is not None:\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\n    # Find root\n    root_id = None\n    for node in nodes:\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = node['id']\n            break\n    if root_id is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"No root node (parent null)\")\n    # Initialize retained set and symbol counts\n    retained = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Process nodes in order: deepest first, tie by id\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removed = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        # Check if node has any retained children\n        if any(child in retained for child in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        # Check if removing would eliminate a required symbol\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\n            continue\n        # Remove\n        removed.append(nid)\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n    # Sort retained\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': removed,\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\n    }\n\nThat's it.\n\nTest with first case mentally above matches.\n\nEdge: ensure that if a node's parent is removed before we process the node? But we process deepest first, so child will be considered before parent. So parent may have children that are already removed, so check for retained children will pass because no children retained. Good.\n\nNow format as JSON.\n\nImplementation string: need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWrite as: \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\" etc.\n\nI'll produce the final JSON.\n\nCheck output schema: exactly two keys.\n\nAlso note: the task says \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`.\" The output of the solve function should be a JSON-serializable object as per schema. That is fine.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":3244,"total_tokens":5416,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2822},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}